You can prefetch a deferred block's dependencies by specifying a **prefetch trigger** in the `@defer` block's trigger expression. This trigger loads the JavaScript for the deferred content before it is actually shown, allowing you to start prefetching resources early (such as when the browser becomes idle) so that they are ready faster when the content is rendered.

To specify a prefetch trigger, you prefix it with the keyword `prefetch` and separate it from the block's main trigger by a semicolon (`;`). For example, if you want to prefetch when the browser is idle but only render the deferred block on user interaction, you write:

```html
@defer (on interaction; prefetch on idle) {
  <large-cmp />
} @placeholder {
  <div>Large component placeholder</div>
}
```

You can also customize the idle timeout for prefetching, e.g., start prefetching after 500 milliseconds of idle time:

```html
@defer (on interaction; prefetch on idle(500)) {
  <large-cmp />
}
```

This way, the dependencies of the `@defer` block are prefetched before rendering, improving perceived performance by loading resources before the user interacts with the content `[angular-prefetching-data-with-prefetch-146-0][angular-prefetching-data-with-prefetch-146-1]`.

### Note on deferrable dependencies:
Only standalone components, directives, pipes, and their CSS styles declared inside the `@defer` block can be deferred and prefetched. Also, they cannot be referenced outside the defer block in the same file to qualify for deferral `[angular-which-dependencies-are-deferred-137-0]`.

---

**Sources:** `[angular-prefetching-data-with-prefetch-146-0][angular-prefetching-data-with-prefetch-146-1][angular-which-dependencies-are-deferred-137-0]`

To create a two-way bindable input on a component, follow these steps:

1. Define a **model input** property in your component using `model()`. This is similar to a standard input but allows the component to write new values that propagate back to the parent when using two-way binding.

```ts
@Component(/* ... */)
export class CustomSlider {
  // Define a model input named "value".
  value = model(0);

  increment() {
    // Update the model input, propagating the value to any bindings.
    this.value.update(oldValue => oldValue + 10);
  }
}
```

2. In the parent's template, bind to the model input using the banana-in-a-box syntax (parentheses inside square brackets), e.g.:

```html
<custom-slider [(value)]="volume"></custom-slider>
```

This two-way binding keeps the `value` inside the `CustomSlider` and the `volume` property in the parent `MediaControls` component in sync.

This setup allows the custom component to both receive an initial value and propagate changes back to the parent, enabling two-way data binding on a plain property.

References:

- Defining and updating a model input property with `model()` and `.update()` method enables two-way binding from the component side. `[angular-model-inputs-57-0]`
- Usage of banana-in-a-box syntax `[(value)]` in the parent component template creates the two-way binding from the parent's side. `[angular-two-way-binding-with-plain-properties-58-0]`
- Model inputs are specifically recommended to enable two-way binding for components that modify a value based on user interaction, such as custom form controls. `[angular-when-to-use-model-inputs-61-0]`

**Sources:** `[angular-model-inputs-57-0][angular-two-way-binding-with-plain-properties-58-0][angular-when-to-use-model-inputs-61-0]`

To stop the router from setting an input to `undefined` when the value was never present in the router data for the active component instance, you should set the `unmatchedInputBehavior` option to `'undefinedIfStale'` in your router configuration. This option prevents the router from setting `undefined` for inputs that have never been available, thus retaining their initial values unless explicitly provided by the router.

Use this configuration:

```ts
provideRouter(
  appRoutes,
  withComponentInputBinding({
    unmatchedInputBehavior: 'undefinedIfStale',
  }),
);
```

Additionally, if you want inputs to retain their initial values and disable query parameter binding, combine `unmatchedInputBehavior: 'undefinedIfStale'` with `queryParams: false`:

```ts
provideRouter(
  appRoutes,
  withComponentInputBinding({
    queryParams: false,
    unmatchedInputBehavior: 'undefinedIfStale',
  }),
);
```

Note the exception: matrix parameters, if removed after being provided, will still cause the input to be set to `undefined` to avoid stale data retention.

This configuration ensures inputs retain initial values and are not set to `undefined` unless stale data is detected or values are explicitly removed in navigation.

**Sources:**  
[angular-configure-behavior-for-inputs-not-available-in-router-data-473-0]  
[angular-configure-behavior-for-inputs-not-available-in-router-data-473-1]

To ignore unknown elements in a nested component test instead of declaring them, you can use stub components for the nested components you don't want to test in detail. This avoids the need to declare the real nested components, their nested components, and services they may inject.

Specifically, for components or directives that play little or no role in the test, create stub versions with matching selectors but empty templates and empty classes. For example:

```ts
@Component({selector: 'app-banner', template: ''})
class BannerStub {}

@Component({selector: 'router-outlet', template: ''})
class RouterOutletStub {}

@Component({selector: 'app-welcome', template: ''})
class WelcomeStub {}
```

Then declare those stub components in the test module setup (e.g., using `TestBed.overrideComponent` to override the `imports` of your component under test):

```ts
TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  providers: [provideRouter([]), UserAuthentication],
}).overrideComponent(App, {
  set: {
    imports: [RouterLink, BannerStub, RouterOutletStub, WelcomeStub],
  },
});
```

This replaces the unknown elements with minimal stub versions so they can be ignored in the test without causing Angular compiler errors about unrecognized tags or requiring you to declare the real nested components and their dependencies. This technique helps keep your tests focused only on the primary component. 

In summary, instead of declaring the real nested components, you stub them out with dummy components and include those in the test module configuration to ignore the unknown elements in nested component tests.

Sources: [angular-nested-component-tests-603-0][angular-nested-component-tests-603-1][angular-stubbing-unneeded-components-604-0]
